---
// Lesson 2.2a — Spikes: Collisions, Health & Death
// OnCollisionEnter2D vs trigger; the death loop in StatManager.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>2.2a — Spikes: Collisions, Health &amp; Death</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Coins were a ghost passing through a wall; spikes are a wall. Same
    meeting, different physics — and today you also meet the death
    loop: the most consequential six lines in the starter project.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Predict First</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Read the spike half of PlayerController.cs:</p>
    <pre><code>private void OnCollisionEnter2D(Collision2D collision)
{
    if (collision.gameObject.CompareTag("Spikes"))
    {
        if (StatManager.manager != null)
        {
            StatManager.manager.ChangeHealth(-1);
        }
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p>Notice what changed vs yesterday: a different method
    (<strong>OnCollisionEnter2D</strong>, not OnTriggerEnter2D), a
    different parameter type (<strong>Collision2D</strong>, not
    Collider2D), and — this is the part to predict — the coin vanishes
    when you touch it, but the spikes do NOT destroy you. You slide off
    them. What is different about the Spikes prefab's collider that
    makes Unity call a collision instead of a trigger?</p>

    <p>Then find the answer in the Inspector: select the Spikes prefab
    and look at its BoxCollider2D — <strong>Is Trigger</strong> is
    unchecked. Same component, one checkbox, two different events.</p>

    <div class="callout warn">
        <strong>The rule, once and forever:</strong> a trigger lets
        things pass through (coins, pickups, ladders — "touching means
        something"); a solid collider stops things (spikes, walls,
        floors — "bumping means something"). If your handler never
        fires, check which checkbox the collider has before touching a
        single line of code.
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — rebuild the damage.</strong> Comment out the
    spike branch and retype it: collide → tag → ChangeHealth(-1). Run:
    watch the Console count 3 → 2 as you bump spikes. Then die on
    purpose.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — read the death loop.</strong> In
    StatManager.cs:</p>
    <pre><code>public void ChangeHealth(int amount)
{
    health += amount;
    Debug.Log("Health Changed! New Health: " + health);

    if (health &lt;= 0)
    {
        Debug.Log("Player Died!");
        HandleDeath();
    }
}

private void HandleDeath()
{
    // Halve coins rounded down
    coins = Mathf.FloorToInt(coins / 2f);
    health = 3;
    UnityEngine.SceneManagement.SceneManager.LoadScene("Start");
}</code></pre>

    <p>
        Trace the consequence chain out loud: spike → ChangeHealth(-1)
        → health 0 → HandleDeath → coins cut in half (rounded down),
        health restored, scene reset. Death has a <strong>cost</strong>
        — half your coins. That is game design: not a game-over screen,
        a tax.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — where does health live?</strong> Select the
    StatManager object in the Hierarchy and watch the Inspector while
    you spike yourself: health ticks 3 → 2 → 1 in the Inspector —
    <em>live</em>, in play mode. The number you see is the number
    ChangeHealth mutates.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Hurt yourself on purpose</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>What happens when you land on two spike tiles at once?
                One −1 or several? Why does the answer depend on how
                the tiles are built?</li>
            <li>Change the spikes' collider to Is Trigger. What breaks
                — and which method would you have to switch to in
                order to fix it?</li>
            <li>Set starting health to 1 in the StatManager's
                Inspector. How fast is death now — and is 1 too
                punishing for the Overworld? Argue one sentence.</li>
            <li>In HandleDeath, comment out the LoadScene line. What
                happens at zero health instead — and what did the
                scene reset actually protect you from?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Design the damage</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Spikes cost 1 heart. Design a trap that costs 2 — what
                changes in code, what changes in the scene, and where
                would a new tag go?</li>
            <li>Death halves your coins. Why "rounded down" — what
                would 1 coin halve into, and what does
                FloorToInt(0.5) actually produce?</li>
            <li>Hard mode: a spike tile that only hurts if you touch
                the TOP of it. Could a single if-statement inside
                OnCollisionEnter2D do it? (Hint: <code>collision</code>
                knows more than the tag — explore what it carries.)</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Say when Unity calls OnTriggerEnter2D vs OnCollisionEnter2D — and why</li>
            <li>Walk through the death loop line by line: health, coins, scene</li>
            <li>Explain why death is a coin tax instead of a game-over</li>
            <li>Check a collider's Is Trigger box before blaming the code</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/triggers-vs-collisions.html">Triggers vs Collisions</a> — the full reference with the trap table</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/index.html">Unit 2: Stats &amp; Pickups</a> — the StatManager in depth</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="2.3a.html">2.3a — Health Potions &amp; the Max-Health Cap</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="2.2a" title="2.2a Spikes: Collisions, Health &amp; Death — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
